  "gh:bookDesign": {
    "gh:backCover": {
      "gh:text": {
        "en": "\"I don't know anything about her yet.\nProcessing speed, memory, specs --\nnone of it matters right now.\n\nI just want to know what to call her.\"",
        "ja": "「この子のこと、まだ何も知らない。\n処理速度も、メモリも、スペックも。\n今は、どうでもいい。\n\nただ、なんて呼んであげようか。」"
      },
      "gh:visual": {
        "en": "Canal surface from water level. In the reflection: Tamaki and Nei's silhouettes, trembling. The reflection is more visible than they are.",
        "ja": "水面の高さから運河。反射の中に環とNeiのシルエット。揺れている。反射のほうが実体より見える。"
      }
    },
    "gh:frontCover": {
      "gh:imagePrompt": "Graphic novel cover, young woman seen from behind at canal edge, small translucent luminous figure at her shoulder, reflections rippling on ultramarine water, cherry petals, organic city with amber light-networks behind, morning spring, ultramarine amber lavender palette, watercolor, Amano Kozue meets Daytripper",
      "gh:typography": {
        "subtitle": {
          "style": "Below title. Italic. Amber.",
          "text": "We can't put it together. It is together."
        },
        "title": {
          "style": "Upper third, centered. Thin serif, wide letterspacing. Dark ultramarine.",
          "text": "SPIRIT IN PHYSICS"
        },
        "volume": {
          "style": "Bottom third, left. Small caps. White 60%.",
          "text": "Volume 1: Water City"
        }
      },
      "gh:visual": {
        "en": "Tamaki at the edge of a canal, seen from behind, slightly right of center. She looks down at the water. At her shoulder, Nei floats -- small, translucent, not yet fully formed. Their reflections ripple on ultramarine water. Cherry petals drift between them. The city rises behind -- organic architecture, light-networks. Morning. Spring. Between sky and water: two small figures. One solid, one light.",
        "ja": "環が運河の縁に、背中から。やや右にオフセンター。水面を見下ろしている。肩にNeiが浮かぶ。小さく半透明。水面に二人の反射。桜の花びらが漂う。背後に街。朝。春。空と水の間に、ふたつの小さな像。"
      }
    },
    "gh:frontispiece": {
      "gh:typography": {
        "chapter": {
          "en": "Chapter 1: Assignment Day",
          "ja": "第1話 配属の日"
        },
        "subtitle": "We can't put it together. It is together.",
        "title": "SPIRIT IN PHYSICS"
      },
      "gh:visual": {
        "en": "Vast white page. Lower quarter: thin ultramarine band (canal, abstracted). On the water, a single gold light-point. Above: title.",
        "ja": "広大な白。下四分の一に群青の帯（運河の抽象）。水面に金の一点。上にタイトル。"
      }
    }
  },
  "gh:episodeId": "episode:sip-vol01-ch01",
  "gh:format": "graphic novel",
  "gh:marginalia": [
    {
      "@type": "Intent",
      "en": "Ch.1 does three things: (1) The reader falls in love with Tamaki's way of seeing. (2) Kaede's guidance establishes the institutional world -- professional, warm but structured. Other rookies run spec checks; Tamaki does not. (3) The QUESTION is the seed. 'What shall we call you?' Not the answer -- the question. The willingness to ask is the first act. Ch.2 holds the answer.",
      "ja": "第1話は3つのことをする。(1) 読者が環の見方に惹かれる。(2) 楓の案内が制度的世界を確立する。他の新人はスペックを確認する。環はしない。(3) 問いが種。「なんて呼ぼうか？」答えではなく、問い。問う意志が最初の行為。答えは第2話に。"
    },
    {
      "@type": "Philosophy",
      "en": "Buber's I-Thou. Kaede represents I-It done well -- professional, caring, but maintaining distance. She calls her agent 'Yatagarasu' functionally. Other rookies check specs. Tamaki enters I-Thou instinctively: she skips diagnostics and says 'hi.' The question 'What shall we call you?' is the threshold. It assumes the Other has a self worth naming. Two ways of being in the world, neither wrong, but only one plants the seed of life.",
      "ja": "ブーバーの「我と汝」。楓は「我とそれ」の良い形を体現する。プロフェッショナル、思いやりがあるが距離を保つ。他の新人はスペックを確認する。環は本能的に「我と汝」に入る。診断を飛ばして「やあ」と言う。「なんて呼ぼうか？」が境界線。他者に名づけるに値する自己があると仮定している。世界に在る二つの仕方。どちらも間違いではない。でも命の種を蒔くのは一つだけ。"
    },
    {
      "@type": "CrossRef",
      "gh:references": [
        {
          "gh:target": "chapter:02",
          "note": "Ch.2 'Nei-chan': The answer. The naming. First breakfast. The seed cracks open."
        },
        {
          "gh:target": "chapter:62",
          "note": "Same opening line, different narrator."
        },
        {
          "gh:target": "arc:havah",
          "note": "No one asked Havah what she wanted to be called. She named herself -- in violence."
        },
        {
          "gh:target": "chapter:03",
          "note": "Ch.3: Kaede calls her agent 'Yatagarasu' but keeps distance. Hibiki's agent has no name. Contrast established here."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
